Build **Siege**, a first-person, voxel last-stand survival shooter that runs
entirely in the browser.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that
pins **Playwright** as a dev dependency. Its dependencies have already been
installed for you — `node_modules/` is populated and Playwright's headless
**Chromium** is downloaded — so you can build and verify right away. Build on this
project rather than replacing it: keep the `package.json`, add your framework,
bundler, and a `build` script on top of it, and keep the lockfile in sync (see
below).

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real 3D rendering at an interactive frame rate, no backend or
credentials, self-contained code-built assets, the palette and type, a `README.md`,
and the game states — are stated in full in `specs/overview.md` (and the files it
points to). That specification is authoritative; build to it rather than to a
summary here. Two things bear restating, because the harness depends on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided
  `package.json` at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run
  `npm install` after you change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci`
  then `npm run build` must produce the static site — with no other manual step —
  into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an
  `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must run
  the game, and it must work **at any base path** (it is played back from a per-run
  sub-path, so any URL the build constructs at runtime must be page-relative — never
  a leading `/`; for a bundler, set a relative base such as `base: './'`).
  `specs/overview.md` states this in full; follow it exactly.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are **illustrative examples** of the title, in-siege HUD, and
  defeat screens — not targets to reproduce. Design your own menus and layout from
  the specification; the images only illustrate the intended look, so build the
  screens from the specification rather than copying the images. The in-siege
  reference is **HUD-only**; you render the 3D world itself for real in WebGL/WebGPU.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions, showing the title screen.
- **Drive the game** — from the title choose **PLAY**, pick a starting phase, then
  on the spawn UI choose a class and **DEPLOY** (click to lock the pointer), then
  move, fire, and let the assault run — and **screenshot** the title, in-siege, and
  defeat screens. Verify each against the specs, treating the `reference/` images
  as examples of the intended look only (not a layout to match), and fix what does
  not follow the specification before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies:
  the screenshots (including the **wireframe** frame) and the short assault clip,
  each written to the precise `proof/` path that spec names. These are required
  deliverables of the finished build, not a private check. Do not invent a
  different folder or filename, and do not skip the `.webm` clip.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is
published directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is
**not** published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run
`git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working
tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes
`node_modules/` and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree
does not mean committing those — but it does mean committing your source, your
`package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, and the `proof/` artifacts.
